// tuples.mn — Tuple primitives for the Mentl runtime
//
// Layout: [field0:i32][field1:i32]...[fieldN-1:i32]
// Exactly 4 bytes per slot. No length prefix — arity is static per type
// and is known at compile time. The emitter passes arity as a literal.
//
// Tuples are structurally distinct from lists:
//   - No tag word. No length header. No tree variants.
//   - Arity fixed at construction. Random access is a single i32.load.
//   - make_tuple / tuple_set / tuple_get are the complete surface.
//
// A tuple IS a positional product (L1 value-ontology), so its ULTIMATE form is
// the SAME width-summed product machinery records use (emit_field_stores_acc /
// field_byte_offset): an (f64, i32) tuple is 12 unboxed bytes, an f64 element
// indexed by a constant is an f64.load at its width-summed offset — never a
// store_i32 truncating every slot. These uniform-i32 primitives are the all-i32
// instance of that product layout (byte-identical, Law 7); they DISSOLVE into
// the record path when construction + index + match-bind migrate in lock-step.
// Named peer: Hβ.lower.tuple-as-product.
//
// Effects declared in std/compiler/types.mn — not re-declared here.
import types

// make_tuple — allocate `arity` 4-byte slots (uninitialized).
// Callers fill slots immediately via tuple_set before the tuple escapes.
fn make_tuple(arity) = alloc(arity * 4)

// Access
//
// tuple_set — store val into slot idx (0-indexed). Returns the tuple
// pointer so calls can be chained:
//   make_tuple(2) |> tuple_set(0, a) |> tuple_set(1, b)
fn tuple_set(t, idx, val) = {
  store_i32(t + idx * 4, val)
  t
}

// tuple_get — load the value at slot idx.
fn tuple_get(t, idx) = load_i32(t + idx * 4)
